  {
   "recommendationid": "232917945",
   "author": {
    "steamid": "76561198275723144",
    "personaname": "Beetleboom",
    "persona_status": "offline",
    "profile_url": "https://steamcommunity.com/profiles/76561198275723144/",
    "num_games_owned": 0,
    "num_reviews": 3,
    "playtime_forever": 775,
    "playtime_last_two_weeks": 44,
    "playtime_at_review": 769,
    "last_played": 1787680085,
    "avatar": "b522ae944c61d73581b7baf55c4154cb7b323277"
   },
   "language": "english",
   "review": "While on paper, blender would seem to be a marvelous program, its much less impressive in practice. Not only is it incredibly user-unfriendly (it expects users to have an encyclopedic knowledge of all of its features right out the gate to use the program), but for whatever reason, using it for more than 5ish minutes lags my whole computer, even after closing the program and trying to do other things, until I reboot the device or restart my graphics drivers. Yes, there are decades of forum posts from users and video guides online, but I shouldn't have to consult outside sources to do extremely basic things, nor should my PC start lagging from having maybe 40 polygons MAX from an unfinished model I was working on nearly brick my computer. Program could easily be a 10/10 if they sent you a damn textbook as the user manual upon downloading or automatically enrolled all of its prospective users in some semester-long course on how to use it, but as it stands, it's unfortunately more of a hassle to use than anything. Yes, its technically versatile, and I appreciate that it isn't locked behind a paywall, but nobody is gonna be able to use this program to make anything of their own unless they've sunk hours of their life reading and studying how to use the program. Other free modelling programs, albeit more limited, exist, and in my opinion, are far better for casual 3d modelers,",
   "timestamp_created": 1786827366,
